{title: When I Survey the Wondrous Cross}
{artist: Isaac Watts}
{key: Eb}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 72}

Verse 1
When [Eb]I sur[Ab]vey the [Eb]wondrous [Bb]cross
on [Eb]which the [Ab]Prince of [Bb]glory [Eb]died,
my [Bb]richest [Eb]gain I [Ab]count but [Eb]loss,
and [Ab]pour con[Eb]tempt on [Bb]all my [Eb]pride.

Verse 2
Forbid it, Lord,
that I should
boast, Save in the death
of Christ my
God! All the vain things
that charm
me most, I
sacrifice them to
His blood.

Verse 3
See from His head,
His hands, His
feet, Sorrow and love
flow mingled down!
Did e'er such love
and sorrow
meet, Or thorns compose
so rich a
crown?

Verse 4
His dying crimson,
like a
robe, Spreads o'er His body
on the tree;
Then I am dead
to all
the globe, And
all the globe
is dead to
me.

Verse 5
Were the whole realm
of nature
mine, That were a present
far too small;
Love so amazing,
so divine, Demands my soul,
my life, my
all.
